european countries have begun imposing  new restrictions on public  and private life as they struggle to  cope with steep rises  in coronavirus infections the uk which  has  europe's highest pandemic death toll has  unveiled a new three-tier system  closing pubs gyms and betting shops in  the highest risk areas  and the regional government in northern  ireland has just imposed europe's  harshest second wave lockdown  restrictions the czech republic already  imposed a state of emergency today  new restrictions come into place closing  all schools bars restaurants and clubs  and the netherlands is also returning to  partial lockdown closing restaurants  hotels and cafes for two weeks  it was what the hospitality industry was  dreading most  a return to lockdown from midnight  restaurants bars and cafes  in the netherlands must shut their doors  for four weeks  masks too will now be mandatory in  indoor public spaces  the government says it was left with  little choice in the face of spiraling  infection rates  it's a hard message and the new rules  are going to hurt  but it's the only way because the facts  don't lie we have to be stricter on  ourselves  and stricter on our behavior  it's a similar picture at the forefront  of europe's second  wave in the czech republic testing has  been ramped up across the country  and tighter measures have been enforced  as well as the leisure industries  schools will be shut for three weeks  masks at tram stops  now a must officials conceded they may  have relaxed rules too  quickly in the summer  i wasn't strong enough in the summer and  i didn't insist on measures to protect  us from the second wave  meanwhile in france business owners in  the capital threw a cocktail party  protest  to draw attention to their plight  bars are in the middle of a two-week  closure that officials hope would stem  the rising number of infections  it hasn't and the public is bracing for  new restrictions to be announced  the government needs to put in place  strong and powerful measures  in line with the economic tragedy that  we're facing here and across france  across europe growing infection rates  fresh lockdown measures  and the inevitable health and economic  hardship to follow  is the headache for which there are no  easy fixes  well for more let's bring in dw's band  reget he's following this story for us  in brussels bent  talk us through these european traffic  lights how's it supposed to work  the european traffic light has four  colors actually red  for dangerous zones orange for medium  infection rates and green  for countries and regions which are okay  and gray for those countries  who don't give the data to the ecdc the  european center of disease control in  stockholm that will publish  this new map of europe not only of the  countries but  the regions and this is new broken down  to 200 regions  and the new point is that not only the  incidences so the new infection  cases will be rated but also  the number of tests and the positive  tests that are returned so you have a  new criteria to  to apply and that might change the map  a little bit for example in germany the  number 50 incidences  is no longer valid it can arrange  between 25 to 150 incidences  but you also have to apply the positive  return rate of tests  which should hover around four percent  and not higher  and all these measures are designed only  for tourism travel  essential travel and urgent business  is not afflicted by this and all these  measures  are voluntarily so the member states are  not obliged to take part in this new  traffic light system okay well this  system does sound like it could provide  some valuable orientation to travelers  uh  how soon could it be put in place  but it could be in place actually next  week because the data are all there  and the ecdc the european center for  disease control will publish this  on the website reopen.europe.eu  and then everybody can see it but the  measures that follow out of this new map  these new indicators  are also up to the member states so they  can decide if they will put in place a  new lockdown  curfews or the obligation  to go into quarantine this is all up to  the member states  and while europe waits on that traffic  light scheme infection numbers continue  to rise  what are the main hot spots about right  now  well according to the newest numbers and  i have to look on this map  on the map here in my map it's um  western europe is mostly affected so  spain france  the united kingdom which are the current  hotspots also belgium here in brussels  but now check the czech republic on the  rise and also the netherlands again and  you can see  also a surge in bulgaria and romania  and according to this new traffic like  system only finland  and parts of eastern germany would be in  the green zone all the rest is orange or  red  and the tendency is that the cases are  going up  but in difference to the first wave  mortality hospitalization  and also excess mortality are fairly  lower  but the trend is upwards and now it's  the problem how to  to flatten the curve again most of  europe orange or red  bantry in brussels thanks so much  well the united kingdom has recorded  europe's highest pandemic  death toll and new infections there are  growing at an  alarming rate especially in the north of  england prime minister boris johnson has  outlined a three-tier system  reinstating restrictions in high-risk  areas dw charlotte chelsea pill reports  from liverpool and manchester  it's last orders for this pub in the  northern english city liverpool  after a three and a half month lock down  earlier this year pubs and bars here are  once again being told to close their  doors  new restrictions are coming into place  across the north of england bringing a  new wave of darkness for both businesses  and customers i got very depressed over  a shutdown and i've only started picking  up since books started opening again  i'll do that and see my friends have a  couple of beers  chill you know it's heartbreaking  it's a place to escape to if you like  you know apart a long time apart elaine  died in april  with this covered she had her health  issues  within two weeks she was gone i lived by  myself i  got depression and stuff so we've been  supporting each other  it does feel as though we're being  punished like many landlords here connor  foley says even paying rent will become  difficult  after days of uncertainty he thinks the  government in london's failing  businesses  in the north i think it's quite  disgraceful  how they've let people fear the worst  without  knowing what help they will get not even  speaking to local leaders until  the very last minute i think it's just a  horrible way  to govern in normal times never mind  when everyone's afraid of what happens  next  but there's no getting away from the  case numbers liverpool has one of the  highest covered rates in europe  alarmingly the uk now has more hospital  admissions than before it went into  lockdown in march  and it's inevitable other cities will  follow in liverpool's footsteps  before anybody enters the restaurant we  have a temperature gauge so we'll just  check your temperature  neighboring manchester award-winning  restaurant owner helen c is doing  everything she can  to keep her doors open but she's living  every day on a knife edge  i didn't know whether to order stock for  this week if i'm honest  um i didn't know whether to take  bookings  i didn't even know whether i should open  up or not you can't operate  when things are so uncertain you don't  know whether you're going to be open or  closed tomorrow  the lady in red is my grandmother lily  kwok  and she opened one of the first chinese  restaurants in manchester even helen and  her sisters are third generation  restauranteurs  they're now facing an unprecedented  challenge we're already struggling  if i'm honest it's not just me carrying  the burden it's the entire family  that's a lot of pressure and i don't  want to let them down i don't want to  let my customers down  and yet with the trading conditions  i might have no choice but with every um  you know order  we write them a little note we call them  lockdown letters  takeaway boxes are now one of just a few  rays of light for restauranteurs like  helen  we still have a whole lot of life to  live  and a whole lot of examples to set a  message of hope to a community holding  its breath  for more bad news  well those are just some of the latest  curbs  other measures are already in place in  several european countries  spain for example has a state of  emergency in the madrid region to  enforce a partial lockdown  affecting four and a half million people  masks are now  mandatory in all public places france  has placed paris marseille  and seven other large cities under  maximum virus alert  closing bars gyms swimming pools and  banning public parties  cases are also rising rapidly here in  germany since friday  new nightlife restrictions are in effect  in the capital berlin bars must close at  11  p.m further hitting berlin's famously  liberal nightlife  economy chancellor angela merkel will  meet leaders of germany's federal states  shortly  to try to solve the confusion around 16  different  sets of rules the  incidence of new coronavirus cases in  germany keeps rising  every day new cities join the list of  risk areas  some areas in germany have surpassed the  key threshold of 50 infections per 100  000 inhabitants over the last seven days  including major cities such as frankfurt  berlin  munich and cologne as infections climb  germany is trying to figure out how to  deal with the new upsurge  one problem the solutions differ all  over the country  in berlin for example there are no  limitations for travelers from  inner german risk areas staying in  hotels  there is also no obligation to wear  masks on the streets  another city another set of rules in  munich  travelers from designated risk areas can  only stay at a hotel  if they show a recent negative covet  test and masks are mandatory in some  outdoor areas of the city  the different rules are making it hard  for germans to understand what they can  and cannot do  in essence no one can really remember  the rules  they seem to be pointless and i don't  think they will survive for a long  period of time because  more and more cities are going beyond  the threshold which is  let's say the thresholds the alarm  threshold which is in use here  so the policy becomes redundant in a  couple of weeks anyway  but the end of that chaos might be near  the leaders of germany's 16 federal  states arrived in berlin  and are set to meet with chancellor  angela merkel for the first non-virtual  meeting in  months some state premiers have already  announced they will cling to the  accommodation ban  while others want to see it dropped the  question  is will the chancellor's personal and  in-person authority  be enough to unify the positions  i want to put that question to our chief  political correspondent linda crane  milinda can the chancellor  wrangle unified measures from all those  16 regional leaders i think it's quite  likely  that she can she's known as an adept  negotiator and it will definitely make a  difference  that the state premiers are meeting  personally with her  for the first time in a number of months  and there is  precedent uh for broken brokering such  an agreement if we look back to the  first wave of corona here in germany  in mid-march april there was quite a bit  of uh discrepancy and differences of  opinion  among the different federal states but  under the watchful eye of the chancellor  at that time  they did manage to agree on a unified  approach and the fact is there's  increasing recognition  that the lack of consistency this  patchwork of different  rules does confuse citizens and  therefore  discourage compliance so today we're  hearing from two of the most powerful  state premiers the heads of north  rhine-westphalia on the one hand  and bavaria on the other whose  approaches often  differ that they do now think it is time  for a unified set of rules well  infection rates are rising rapidly  really across the country are we going  to see a unified approach with tighter  restrictions then  yes i think that we will see tighter  restrictions for example  in regard to the wearing of masks  perhaps  extending the number of locations where  masks must  be worn perhaps also some tighter limits  on  public gatherings and and social  events but that might be paired with at  least a partial roll back  on controversial measures whose efficacy  has been questioned by authorities and  that would certainly include  this travel ban that has been imposed by  some rural parts of germany on  people coming from hot spots from bigger  cities  requiring them to show a negative corona  test  before being allowed to stay in local  hotels  authorities say that that doesn't make  sense especially with testing capacity  increasingly limited melinda germany  came  out of phase one of this pandemic  looking rather good how is it looking  for phase two  well people here are definitely in  agreement that  this is a decisive moment for the  country both the government spokesman  saying  earlier this week that germany is at the  beginning of a second  wave and that this now is a crucial  moment in time  and the head of germany's uh leading uh  corona  virus institute the the public  health authority of robert koch  institute says as well that this moment  is decisive and what really counts is  individual responsibility across the  board so we'll see if that  responsibility starts now with this  meeting of the state premiers our chief  political correspondent  melinda crane thank you melinda  you

